rm\": \"no\",\n      \"substitution\": \"no\",\n      \"protection\": \"unclear\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"none\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Wolpe appears to accept Harris's description of doubts being recast as sharing Jesus's suffering (E1013–1025), then defends religious service and benefits. That could endorse converting contrary experience into confirmation. However, E1026–1047 may defend religion's usefulness instead of that inference; explicit adoption of the protective device remains uncertain.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1172,\n      \"endEvent\": 1196,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 1122,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 1222,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"it can even be diminished in this world but it doesn't mean that it's diminished forever\",\n      \"form\": \"no\",\n      \"substitution\": \"no\",\n      \"protection\": \"unclear\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"none\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Answering brain-damage evidence at E1131–1153, Wolpe allows diminished identity while proposing persistence in an unknown state. This may shield survival from contrary neurological patterns. However, he presents a conditional possibility, distinguishes consciousness from particular abilities, and accepts scientific exploration (E1172–1179); unrestricted immunity is not clearly asserted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 2387,\n      \"endEvent\": 2413,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 2338,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 2425,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"his experience of religion is not to be evaluated scientifically\",\n      \"form\": \"no\",\n      \"substitution\": \"no\",\n      \"protection\": \"yes\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"identity\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Wolpe expressly excludes scientific evaluation of Collins's experience and praises that exclusion as spiritual largeness. The preceding challenge concerns its warrant for Jesus's divinity (E2371–2383), renewed at E2414–2417. His poetry analogy supplies an argument, not a slogan, but the response protects the experience's evidential role without resolving that distinction.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"contrasts\": [\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 2248,\n      \"endEvent\": 2279,\n      \"speaker\": \"A\",\n      \"quote\": \"I shouldn't have said every marker\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Harris identifies community as a possible alternative explanation for religious well-being findings, retracts an overstatement about Sweden, and acknowledges unfinished research. The criticism is reasoned and expressly qualified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1992,\n      \"endEvent\": 2020,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"it's my job to to argue judaism's Excellence not its superiority\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Wolpe explains this compact formulation through a limited personal commitment, openness to other religious paths, and explicit condemnation of harmful conduct. He declines the superiority claim the question invites; the phrase does not substitute for proof of superiority.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}}
